{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2147 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "fdcf1cac965b4c1623407b3b59f72f2baad84b9ed183d1bda17833643dbdf266" :edb/db-before-t 2146 :edb/db-after-t 2147 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535459 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:31:35.323Z" 13194139535459 true] [17592186175708 1100 "Given that $x=-2$ is a solution to the equation $x^3+8 = 0$, find the remaining solutions." 13194139535459 false] [17592186175708 1100 "Given that $x=-2$ is a solution to the equation $x^3+8 = 0,$ find the remaining solutions." 13194139535459 true] [17592186175708 1145 "Let\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^3+8\n$$\n\nSince $x=-2$ is a root of $f(x)$, then according to the factor theorem $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$.\n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}8; - 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x+2)(x^2-2x+4)\n$$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve\n\n$$\nx^2-2x+4 = 0\n$$\n\nby completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 + 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} + 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= -3 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= (\\sqrt{3}\\textrm{i})^{2} \\\\\nx - 1 &= ± \\sqrt{3}\\textrm{i} \\\\\nx &= 1 ± \\sqrt{3}\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are\n\n$$\nx=-2, \\, 1\\pm \\sqrt3\\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139535459 false] [17592186175708 1145 "Let $f(x) = x^3+8.$ Since $x=-2$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065202/assets/a92f1e322fc91c8388e7969117aca8e616625128d82ee12c88ce108cb6b2637e.png)\n\nThus, $f(x) = (x+2)(x^2-2x+4).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $x^2-2x+4 = 0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+3 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+3 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-3 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-2, \\, 1\\pm \\sqrt3\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139535459 true] [17592186175734 1145 "First, we let\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^3-3x^2+12x-10\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and\n\n$$\nx_1=1+3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nis a root of $f(x)$, the complex conjugate\n\n$$\n\\overline{x_1}=\\overline{1+3\\textrm{i}}=1-3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x)$.\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem,\n$\\big(x-(1+3\\text{i})\\big)\\big(x-(1-3\\text{i})\\big)$\nis a factor of $f(x)$. Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - (1 + 3\\textrm{i}))(x - (1 - 3\\textrm{i})) &=  \\\\\nx^{2} - (1 + 3\\textrm{i} + 1 - 3\\textrm{i})x + (1 + 3\\textrm{i})(1 - 3\\textrm{i}) &=  \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + (1^{2} - (3\\textrm{i})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 2x + 10 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2 - 2x + 10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\nx^{2} - 2x + 10x - 1; x^{2} - 2x + 10x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 12x - 10; x^{2} - 2x + 10x^{3} - 2x^{2} + 10x_(―); x^{2} - 2x + 10x^{3} - 0x^{2} + 02x - 10; x^{2} - 2x + 10x^{3}- 0x^{2} + 02x - 10_(―); x^{2} - 2x + 10x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 12x - 10\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x^2 - 2x + 10)(x-1)\n$$\n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\nx - 1 = 0⟹x = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are\n\n$$\nx=1, 1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139535459 false] [17592186175734 1145 "First, we let $f(x) = x^3-3x^2+12x-10.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and $x_1=1+3\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overline{x_1}=\\overline{1+3\\textrm{i}}=1-3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$\\big(x-(1+3\\text{i})\\big)\\big(x-(1-3\\text{i})\\big)$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(1+3\\text{i}+1-3\\text{i})x+(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+({1}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+10 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2 - 2x + 10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-1 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-2x+10\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+12x-10\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}+2x-10 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,-\\,{x}^{2}+2x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x) = (x^2 - 2x + 10)(x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=1, 1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139535459 true] [17592186175856 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350342 13194139535459 true] [17592186175856 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350363 13194139535459 true] [17592186175856 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350377 13194139535459 true] [17592186175856 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350384 13194139535459 true] [17592186175888 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350349 13194139535459 true] [17592186175888 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350356 13194139535459 true] [17592186175888 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350370 13194139535459 true] [17592186175888 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350387 13194139535459 true] [17592186176098 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535459 true] [17592186176098 1100 "Find all of the solutions to $2x^{3} - 9x^{2} + 20x - 8 = 0$ given that $x = 2 - 2\\textrm{i}$ is a solution." 13194139535459 false] [17592186176098 1100 "Find all of the solutions to $2{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+20x-8=0$ given that $x=2-2\\text{i}$ is a solution." 13194139535459 true] [17592186176098 1145 "First, we let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+20x-8.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=2-2\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{2-2\\text{i}}=2+2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(2-2\\text{i}))(x-(2+2\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(2-2\\text{i}))(x-(2+2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(2-2\\text{i}+2+2\\text{i})x+(2-2\\text{i})(2+2\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+({2}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+8 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-4x+8$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x-1 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-4x+8\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+20x-8\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+16x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}+4x-8 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,{x}^{2}+4x-8}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-4x+8)(2x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=\\frac{1}{2},2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$" 13194139535459 true] [17592186176128 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535459 true] [17592186176128 1100 "Given that $x =-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a solution to the equation $36x^{3} + 9x^{2} + 4x + 1 = 0$, find the remaining solutions." 13194139535459 false] [17592186176128 1100 "Given that $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a solution to the equation $36{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+4x+1=0,$ find the remaining solutions." 13194139535459 true] [17592186176128 1145 "Let $f(x)=36{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+4x+1.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ using synthetic division.\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $36$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-9$ | $0$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $36$ | $0$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(36{x}^{2}+4).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $36{x}^{2}+4=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{x}^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ 36{x}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}.$" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350342 1017 #edb/ref 17592186350343 13194139535459 true] [17592186350342 1089 "q-104497" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350342 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535459 true] [17592186350342 1100 "Given that $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a solution to the equation $3{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+8x-2=0,$ find the remaining solutions." 13194139535459 true] [17592186350342 1113 #uuid "bbb34119-16b7-5bfa-b1df-ad34b590b96b" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350342 1145 "Let $f(x)=9{x}^{3}-21{x}^{2}+24x-6.$ Since $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x-\\frac{1}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-\\frac{1}{3})$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{\\frac{1}{3}} & 3 & -7 & 8 & -2 \\\\  &  & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\  & 3 & -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x-\\frac{1}{3})(3{x}^{2}-6x+6).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $3{x}^{2}-6x+6=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x+6 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-2x+2) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+2 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =(\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=\\frac{1}{3},\\,1\\pm \\text{i}.$" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350343 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350344 13194139535459 true] [17592186350343 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350345 13194139535459 true] [17592186350343 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350346 13194139535459 true] [17592186350343 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350347 13194139535459 true] [17592186350343 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350348 13194139535459 true] [17592186350343 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535459 true] [17592186350343 1041 "selection" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350343 1104 #edb/ref 17592186350344 13194139535459 true] [17592186350343 1110 #uuid "b393ecb0-87e2-5c0d-b32a-76f10eae3e34" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350344 1034 "1\\pm \\text{i}" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350344 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535459 true] [17592186350344 1111 #uuid "455737fa-8f9a-5c53-9c32-0e89354e4b0f" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350345 1034 "-1\\pm \\text{i}" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350345 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535459 true] [17592186350345 1111 #uuid "adde2b37-c2fa-57f9-82a7-11b7925d1c38" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350346 1034 "-\\frac{1}{3}\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350346 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535459 true] [17592186350346 1111 #uuid "1e51a76e-05c7-5669-bcc7-f5f410503352" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350347 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350347 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535459 true] [17592186350347 1111 #uuid "8d8bee53-4dd6-5937-be20-fb0df3a13e79" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350348 1034 "\\pm \\text{i}" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350348 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535459 true] [17592186350348 1111 #uuid "eeba1ff4-0775-53e9-a7fa-0bfccf059128" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350349 1017 #edb/ref 17592186350350 13194139535459 true] [17592186350349 1089 "q-104514" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350349 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535459 true] [17592186350349 1100 "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+20=0$ given that $x=3-\\text{i}$ is a solution." 13194139535459 true] [17592186350349 1113 #uuid "f46539e8-9ace-5c88-b4d4-dec07a27569b" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350349 1145 "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+20.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=3-\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{3-\\text{i}}=3+\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(3-\\text{i}))(x-(3+\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(3-\\text{i}))(x-(3+\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(3-\\text{i}+(3+\\text{i)})x+(3-\\text{i})(3+\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+({3}^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+10 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-6x+10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x+2 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-6x+10\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+20} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{x}^{2}-12x+20 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}-12x+20}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-6x+10)(x+2).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-2,3\\pm \\text{i}.$" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350351 13194139535459 true] [17592186350350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350352 13194139535459 true] [17592186350350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350353 13194139535459 true] [17592186350350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350354 13194139535459 true] [17592186350350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350355 13194139535459 true] [17592186350350 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535459 true] [17592186350350 1041 "selection" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350350 1104 #edb/ref 17592186350351 13194139535459 true] [17592186350350 1110 #uuid "7f4d0c9a-78d1-525e-87e4-8a5e9d36fa42" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350351 1034 "x=-2,3\\pm \\te