186350384 1100 "Given that $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a solution to the equation $4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+100x+25=0,$ find the remaining solutions.\n\n$x=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350384 1113 #uuid "befb63f6-09c7-53f3-a3d5-95104472d1e2" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350384 1145 "Let $f(x)=4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+100x+25.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ using synthetic division.\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $4$ | $1$ | $100$ | $25$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-25$ |\n|  | $4$ | $0$ | $100$ | $0$ |\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(4{x}^{2}+100).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $4{x}^{2}+100=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+100 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =-100 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(5\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm 5\\text{i}.$" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350386 13194139535459 true] [17592186350385 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535459 true] [17592186350385 1041 "field-1" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350385 1104 #edb/ref 17592186350386 13194139535459 true] [17592186350385 1110 #uuid "e7706a19-8962-5c0e-ab22-27413dfcd17e" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350386 1034 "5i" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350386 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535459 true] [17592186350386 1111 #uuid "3ab9c5a9-5607-5462-9e6a-27fcf3281e1a" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350387 1017 #edb/ref 17592186350388 13194139535459 true] [17592186350387 1017 #edb/ref 17592186350390 13194139535459 true] [17592186350387 1089 "q-317986" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350387 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535459 true] [17592186350387 1100 "Determine all the solutions to ${x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+25x-25=0$ given that $x=5\\text{i}$ is a solution. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=\\pm $\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350387 1113 #uuid "177f798f-98fb-53b0-bae4-ba0d79693695" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350387 1145 "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+25x-25.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=5\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{5\\text{i}}=-5\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, $(x-5\\text{i})(x-(-5\\text{i}))$ is a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5\\text{i})(x-(-5\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}+(5\\text{i})(-5\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+25$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & {x}^{2}+25\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+25x-25\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+25x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}\\,-25 \\\\  & \\,\\,{\\,-{x}^{2}\\,\\,-25}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+25)(x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=1$ and $x=\\pm \\,5\\text{i}.$" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350388 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350389 13194139535459 true] [17592186350388 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535459 true] [17592186350388 1041 "field-1" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350388 1104 #edb/ref 17592186350389 13194139535459 true] [17592186350388 1110 #uuid "749dd247-28ca-5b74-9f06-5989bc4372ba" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350389 1034 "1" 13194139535459 true] [17592186350389 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535459 true] [17592186350389 1111 #uuid "4ffa7934-b256-534d-9750